# Development & Codebase Change Rules

These rules apply to anyone developing the project and changing the codebase. Their purpose is to keep the codebase easy to navigate, change, and reason about — for both frequent contributors and brand-new developers. They are stack-agnostic and apply throughout development.

**Goal:**
- Anyone opening this repo — veteran or new — can **find where to change behavior quickly** without long searches in the codebase
- The codebase exists for humans who change it often — frequent contributors and new developers.
- Cursor agents and human contributors should follow it when implementing, refactoring, or documenting.
- Optimize for **finding the right file in seconds** and maintaining a structured project folders


---

## 1. One obvious entry point
- The app/entry file only **wires things together** (command registration, route mounting, provider setup, bootstrap).
- All real behavior lives in named domain modules — never hidden in the entry file or in random helper files.

---

## 2. Obvious location
- Code for a feature lives where a developer would look first: group by **domain** or **UI area**, not by file type dumps or "misc".
- Example grouping (adapt to your stack): `auth/`, `billing/`, `api/`, `components/`, `models/`, `services/`.
- If the right folder doesn't exist yet, **propose one** before inventing another flat filename.
- If the right folder does not exist yet, **propose** a target path

---

## 3. Names tell the story
- File, folder, function, and class names match the **product language** and the module's responsibility.
- Avoid cryptic prefixes, duplicate naming, or a jumbled flat namespace.
- Keep naming **consistent** — the same concept gets the same name everywhere.

---

## 4. Small, focused files
- Prefer splitting god files (1000+ lines) over scroll hunts.
- One primary concern per module. When a file does two things, it's time to split.
- Export one primary responsibility per file where possible, and don't mix up unrelated modules and keep the module clean.
- Related code for one feature sits in **one folder or 2–3 adjacent files** in most of the cases when possible
- Prefer **vertical slices** that keep related code together over scattered one-liners across ten files.

---

## 5. Shared logic in one place
- Don't copy-paste the same regex, validation, parsing, or formatting logic across multiple files — fix bugs once, not four times.
- Stateless shared helpers live in a single `shared/`/`utils/`/`common/` module.

---

## 6. Smallest viable diff
- Only change the files needed for the task.
- No global reformatting, no mass renames, no drive-by lint fixes, no unrelated "cleanup" bundled into a feature change.
- Preserve existing patterns. Reuse existing helpers before writing new ones.

---

## 7. Keep the specs up to date
- When you add, move, or rename a module, update the specs folder in the **same change** so the docs never lie.
- Keep the architecture decisions in specs recording **why** key choices were made (stack, patterns, rejected alternatives) to prevent conflicting suggestions later.

---

## 8. Change discipline
- Read the relevant specs before any feature development or planning.
- Break work into small, numbered slices; ship the thinnest vertical slice first.
- Ask before large or ambiguous changes (module boundaries, data formats).
- No automated tests unless explicitly requested — the user verifies manually.
- Explain non-trivial moves in one line if necessary: **where** the code went and **why**.
- **Debugging:** touch only the minimal required files — don't fix unrelated code.
- **Automated tests:** after a fix, give a short (3-line) note on whether an automated test is worth adding — only if absolutely necessary.
- **Refactoring:** if a pattern becomes cluttered after many edits and no longer fits the goal of an easy-to-understand codebase, tell the user — propose the changes, why they're needed, and the benefits — rather than continuing the old format. Refactors and merges must follow standard coding conventions and best practices so the code stays clean and simple.

---

## 9. Code readability (comments & structure)

**Write code for humans first.** It must read top-to-bottom as one clean flow that a new developer can follow without hunting.

### Comments
- Add a **top-of-file comment (1–3 lines)** when the module's role is not obvious from its name.
- Comment **"why", not "what"** — explain the reason and intent behind non-obvious logic, not what the line literally does.
- Comment **only when it adds value**. If the code is self-explanatory, leave it clean instead of over-commenting.

### Structure
- Organize code into clearly separated **logical sections** within a file (e.g. setup, helpers, main flow, exports).
- Keep **one concern per section/function** — don't mix unrelated logic in one block.
- Order code so it reads naturally **top to bottom**: definitions before use, the main flow clearly visible.
- Use **consistent naming and spacing** so the file looks uniform and predictable.

---

## Anti-patterns (avoid these)
| Avoid | Why |
|-------|-----|
| New behavior only in the entry file | Hides logic from search and the specs |
| One flat folder of unrelated files | Namespace becomes unsearchable |
| Copy-paste logic across files | Multiple places to fix the same bug |
| Mega-files without a split plan | New developers can't onboard |
| Moves without updating docs | Docs lie; search fails |
| Scattered one-liner changes across ten files | Hard to review, easy to break |
| Dead code / unused exports left behind | Clutters search and suggestions |


